## Continuation update (2026-09-25; sync-only timeout socket-close heuristic)

- Source review of Java `NettyRemotingClient.invokeSync` and future-based `invoke(String, ...)` found the timeout-close heuristic belongs only to `invokeSync`; async invoke timeouts are instead handled by response-table scanning and do not close the socket. Rust had shared the sync close behavior with its future-based invoke path.
- Split the timeout-close policy in Rust: `invoke_sync` applies the configured Java heuristic (`left > 100ms || left > timeoutMillis / 4`), while regular future and callback invoke timeout leaves the connection open. Extended `p3_u01_inflight_invoke_times_out_when_peer_does_not_reply` to verify a later request succeeds on the same socket after the timed-out request's late response; added `p3_sync_timeout_closes_socket_when_java_heuristic_requires_it` for the sync close behavior.
- Focused timeout tests and the full remoting transport suite (41 cases) PASS; `cargo check -p rocketmq-classic-remoting --locked --offline` PASS. Workspace-wide tests, formatting check, Clippy, metadata checker, differential self-test and diff check remain to be run after this change. No live Broker/Docker validation; Java checkout untouched.
- Updated compatibility inventory and matrix with the scoped timeout policy. Phase 3 remains unaccepted; Phase 4 stays blocked; P3-V04 remains `PENDING_PHASE_19`.
- Follow-up source check confirmed Java evaluates the close heuristic using the timeout remaining after channel acquisition (and compares it with one quarter of the original timeout); Rust now snapshots those same values before admission/response waiting. Full validation PASS: workspace offline tests (76 unit/integration plus doc tests), fmt check, all-target/all-feature Clippy with warnings denied, workspace metadata checker, differential self-test, and `git diff --check`. No Maven rerun or live Broker/Docker test.

