- Compared Java `NettyRemotingClient.invokeOneway` (registers a channel-future listener and returns) with `NettyRemotingAbstract.invokeOnewayImpl` (then acquires semaphore and writes asynchronously). Rust `invoke_oneway` now schedules connection acquisition, hook execution, admission and write on an owned client task and returns after scheduling; post-return errors cannot be reported to the caller, while no Broker receipt/storage guarantee is made.
- Added `p3_oneway_returns_after_scheduling_before_peer_receives_frame`, which proves the call returns while the scripted peer has not yet read the frame and later receives the oneway request. During the edit, the existing V02 peer was observed to race shutdown and lose its expected third accept; reordered that test to join its peer before shutdown. The first full transport run exposed this unrelated test race; focused retest and all 43 transport tests then passed.
- Compared Java `scanAvailableNameSrv` initial/immediate and connect-timeout rescheduling with Rust scanning. Rust now starts immediately (Tokio interval's initial tick), uses skip-on-missed-tick cadence, launches configured address probes concurrently, retains only currently configured addresses, and updates availability only while address remains configured/client is open. Added `p3_nameserver_scan_probes_addresses_concurrently_and_tracks_results`.
- Remaining NameServer differences are explicit: Java per-address scan-executor tasks and ChannelWrapper last-response/active checks are not equivalent to Rust JoinSet probes and timeout scheduler; wrapper replacement/cleanup races remain. Oneway differs at the boundary too: Java initiates/obtains the channel future before registering its listener and returning, while Rust schedules connection acquisition and subsequent work on an owned task; post-return async errors and Java semaphore exception taxonomy also remain differences. A brief attempt to move Rust connection acquisition before scheduling was reverted because it restores the API's wait-for-connect boundary, unlike Java's asynchronous channel-future API; docs retain the exact distinction.
- Focused oneway and scan tests PASS; remoting transport suite PASS (43 tests). The V02 test also asserted its accepted-connection counter before the peer task ran its increment; moved that assertion after `peer.await` to remove the scheduling race. Workspace gates PASS: `cargo test --workspace --locked --offline` (78 unit/integration tests plus doc tests), `cargo fmt --all --check`, workspace all-target/all-feature Clippy with warnings denied, workspace metadata checker, differential self-test, and `git diff --check`. A final shutdown review also prunes completed owned-task handles during task registration; complete workspace tests were rerun successfully after this cleanup. Maven and live Broker/Docker validation were not run. Phase 3 remains unaccepted; Phase 4 stays blocked; P3-V04 remains PENDING_PHASE_19.

## Continuation update (2026-09-25; NameServer removed-address cleanup)

- Compared Java `updateNameServerAddressList` and `closeChannel` with Rust list replacement. Java closes only a channel for the removed address if it is the chosen NameServer; unrelated cached channels remain open. Rust previously closed every removed NameServer connection.
- Changed Rust to close and clear only the removed chosen address's connection. Added `p3_nameserver_update_keeps_non_chosen_removed_connection_open`, using two scripted loopback listeners and public NameServer/channel operations to verify the other cached connection remains alive after configuration removal.
- Compatibility inventory originally recorded that Rust immediately pruned removed availability keys, while Java removes stale keys during scan. Continued Java-source comparison changed Rust to preserve exposed availability until the scan's configured-address retain step; added `p3_nameserver_update_prunes_availability_on_next_scan` to verify the old key remains visible after list replacement and disappears at scan cadence.
- Test/log review also confirmed Java `getAvailableNameSrvList()` returns a snapshot of the availability map without filtering against the configured list, so Rust getter now returns its set as-is. Scan completion no longer rejects probes solely because the configured address list changed, matching the Java task's asynchronous put/remove behavior; a later scan prunes removed entries.
- Updated compatibility matrix and API inventory to reflect the new behavior and retained differences (parallel JoinSet vs Java scan executor; wrapper activity/replacement remain unmatched). Focused regression passed: `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-remoting --test transport p3_nameserver_update_prunes_availability_on_next_scan --locked --offline`.
- More Java branch review found no safe additional semantic changes without altering Rust's public API; oneway post-return failure observability, callback Throwable/error taxonomy, Netty context/channel entry points, Java TLS global-property reload/test-mode/decryption, and Java executor ownership remain non-equivalent or framework-specific and are explicitly retained as parity gaps. No Broker/Docker test; Phase 3 remains unaccepted and Phase 4 blocked. Full workspace validation and a complete P3 acceptance review remain outstanding.


