## TLS parity follow-up (2026-09-25)

- User direction: align Java-observable behavior where representable; for language/Netty differences align logical behavior rather than fabricate framework APIs; align Java trust-all behavior when confirmed in the pinned client source.
- Re-read pinned Java `TlsHelper.buildSslContext(true)` and `TlsSystemConfig`: `tls.test.mode.enable` defaults true and unconditionally selects `InsecureTrustManagerFactory`; with test mode false, `tls.client.authServer=false` also selects trust-all; only test mode false plus authServer true uses configured trust roots/default trust and hostname verification. Thus Java's defaults are trust-all for its client TLS context.
- Implemented explicit Rust `TlsConfig::{test_mode,auth_server}` with matching defaults and behavior. Trust-all verifier bypasses chain and hostname checks while still validating TLS handshake signatures. Verified mode retains custom/native roots and hostname checks. Java-mode tests cover test-mode trust-all and authServer-false trust-all; existing CA/hostname tests cover verified mode and negative cases. `from_ca_file` explicitly selects verified mode as a secure helper expectation.
- Updated compatibility ledger, ADR and README; no TLS properties/config-file reload or password-protected-key decryption added in this change.
- Validation PASS: targeted TLS loopback tests (3); `cargo fmt --all`; `cargo test --workspace --locked --offline` (11 common, 9 protocol, 1 fixture, 11 remoting unit, 4 codec, 51 transport, doc tests); `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features --locked --offline -- -D warnings`; metadata checker; differential comparator self-test; `git diff --check`. No Maven, live Broker or Docker.
- Phase 3 remains NOT ACCEPTED: this closes only the TLS trust-mode behavior gap. Other partial/unmapped Java logical behavior and Phase 19 P3-V04 remain outstanding; Phase 4 stays blocked.

## Callback admission metadata parity (2026-09-26 continuation)

- Java `ResponseFuture.sendRequestOK` initializes to `true`; in `NettyRemotingAbstract.invoke0`, async admission timeout happens before a `ResponseFuture` exists, and the public wrapper constructs a synthetic `ResponseFuture` whose default send status remains true. Rust had reported `false` for all failures before write, conflating initial connection failure with established-channel admission timeout.
- Rust callback metadata now reports send status true when a channel was established and the terminal outcome is a timeout; initial connect/TLS failures and actual send failures remain false. Extended `p3_async_admission_timeout_completes_through_callback` to assert timeout, timed-out flag, and Java-compatible send status.
- Final check exposed an important source detail: `invoke0` may time out in semaphore admission, but after a channel exists and a `ResponseFuture` is created the default is also true; actual write failures explicitly set it false. The corrected Rust condition is intentionally limited to timeout after channel establishment (rather than just admission timeout), preserving that Java field default.
- Targeted regression `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-remoting --test transport p3_async_admission_timeout_completes_through_callback --locked --offline` — PASS. Full workspace gates and final source/plan review remain outstanding. Updated API inventory, compatibility summary and README. Phase 3 remains NOT ACCEPTED; Phase 4 stays blocked; P3-V04 remains pending Phase 19.
- Full gates after this change: `cargo fmt --all --check`; `cargo test --workspace --locked --offline` (87 unit/integration tests: 11 common, 9 protocol, 1 fixture, 11 remoting unit, 4 codec, 51 transport; doc tests passed); `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features --locked --offline -- -D warnings`; workspace metadata checker; differential comparator self-test; `git diff --check` — PASS.
- Re-read both full Phase 3 plans. P3-V01/V02/V03/U01 remain automated offline cases, but the plan requires all observable Java client flows without discrepancies; documented `PARTIAL`/unmapped differences in callbacks/errors, Java/Netty lifecycle and APIs, executor behavior, TLS property/config controls and related branches remain blockers. P3-V04 stays reserved for Phase 19. Phase 3 NOT ACCEPTED; Phase 4 remains blocked. No live Broker, Docker, or Maven run in this continuation.
